  "query_preview": "..Today through Thursday...High pressure over the \nSouthwest remains the dominant factor through the middle of this \nweek. Fully dry conditions are expected with daytime highs around \n5 degrees above seasonal normals. Expect overnight lows closer to \nnormal with efficient radiational cooling each night. Also expect \ngenerally light northeast-east winds 5-15 mph each day, but some \nstronger and localized terrain enhanced winds will be possible \nsouth of the Mogollon Rim.\n\nFriday through Saturday...The aforementioned high pushes eastward \npast Arizona while a pair of low pressure systems dig along the west \ncoast. The first of these systems will dive well south of our area, \nand follow a trajectory through northern Mexico and southern \nArizona. This will bring a slight chances for showers along/south of \nthe Rim and White Mountains with light rainfall accumulations on \nFriday. The second, deeper low pressure system will quickly follow \nbehind on Saturday, following a northern path that will more \ndirectly impact our area. There is still some disagreement among \nensemble guidance on the strength/trajectory of this low, and the \ncurrent forecast calls for a trace-0.3 inches of additional \nrainfall for most of northern Arizona in this second round. \nHowever, some higher-end solutions call for localized accumulations\n0.6 inches or greater. This expected to stay mostly as rainfall, \nthough lower snow levels and some accumulations will be possible \ntowards the higher terrain of far northern Arizona on Saturday \nevening. Besides the rain, expect cooler daytime temperatures and \ngusty winds, especially Saturday.\n\nSunday and onward...A brief return to ridging lead to dry conditions \non Sunday. By Monday-Tuesday, we will see our next round of \nprecipitation as another Pacific low pressure system approaches. \nThere is plenty of spread at this point, so be sure to check back \nfor future updates!",
